# Copy to .env for local runs. CI supplies these from GitHub Secrets.
# Never commit a filled-in copy.

# --- Targets: the deployed dev environment, never localhost ---------------
API_URL=https://dev-api.homechrome.in
ADMIN_URL=https://dev-admin.homechrome.in
STORE_URL=https://dev.homechrome.in

# --- Admin accounts -------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN can refund. OPERATOR must be refused. Both are asserted, so both must
# exist in dev; scripts/cleanup.ts does not create or delete them.
E2E_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@handloom.com
E2E_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
E2E_OPERATOR_EMAIL=operator@handloom.com
E2E_OPERATOR_PASSWORD=

# --- Storefront customer --------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated, and every number must appear in the backend's
# STORE_TEST_PHONES allowlist with STORE_TEST_OTP matching. Exact E.164, no
# spaces — the backend compares with ==, not a prefix.
#
# One number per Playwright worker: a customer has one cart, so the suite runs
# exactly as many workers as there are phones here. Add a number to both lists
# and it parallelises further; leave one and it stays serial. Either
# E2E_STORE_PHONES or E2E_STORE_PHONE is read, so the name can stay singular.
E2E_STORE_PHONE=+919999900001,+919999900002,+919999900003,+919999900004
E2E_STORE_OTP=

# --- PhonePe webhook signature -------------------------------------------
# The refund-settlement specs post to /webhooks/phonepe directly, and the
# handler authenticates with hex SHA256("username:password"). It answers a
# rejection with 200 — PhonePe must not retry a body it cannot authenticate —
# so an unsigned delivery is indistinguishable from an accepted one and the
# specs would pass while testing nothing. Unset, they skip instead.
#
# Must match the deployment's PHONEPE_WEBHOOK_USERNAME / PHONEPE_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD.
E2E_PHONEPE_WEBHOOK_USERNAME=
E2E_PHONEPE_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=

# --- PhonePe sandbox ------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing to configure. Dev points at api-preprod.phonepe.com/apis/pg-sandbox,
# and in UAT the checkout QR encodes an unauthenticated merchant-simulator URL
# rather than a upi:// intent — so the suite scans the QR the way a phone would
# and clicks Success, with no card, VPA or test instrument of any kind.
